 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Storage Complexity:** The algorithm requires the storage of 192 directed vector paths for 4D [S1, unofficial]. This is significantly less than the $2^{16}$ (65,536) entries required for a full lookup table in template-based methods like MCA [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Scalability:** The number of directed paths grows with dimension $N$ as $\prod_{k=2}^N 2k$, while the number of templates for MCA grows as $2^{2^N}$ [S1, unofficial]. This makes STEVE more scalable to higher dimensions than template-based methods.
-*   **Performance:** For a $50 \times 50 \times 50 \times 50$ toxel data set with an iso-value of 1, STEVE in high resolution 'mixed' mode generated 129,563 tetrahedrons [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Minimal data redundancy; avoids multiple counting of manifold supporting points; handles 4D specifically without the exponential blowup of general $N$-D template tables.
-*   **Limitations:** Specific to 4D (or requires extension for higher $N$); topological ambiguities require careful handling to prevent rifts.
+*   **Time and Memory Complexity:** The number of directed paths grows with dimension $N$ as $\prod_{k=2}^N 2k$ [S1, unofficial]. For 4D, this is 192 paths (halved due to symmetry) [S1, unofficial]. In contrast, the number of templates for MCA grows as $2^{2^N}$ [S1, unofficial]. This makes STEVE more scalable to higher dimensions than template-based methods, which would require $2^{16}$ (65,536) configurations for 4D [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Measured Performance:** For a $50 \times 50 \times 50 \times 50$ toxel dataset with an iso-value of 1, STEVE in high resolution 'mixed' mode generated 129,563 tetrahedrons [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Minimal data redundancy by avoiding multiple counting of manifold supporting points within 4D cells [S1, unofficial]. Scalable to higher dimensions where template-based methods become impossible due to resource constraints (e.g., 11-dimensional M-Theory) [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires careful handling of topological ambiguities to prevent rifts [S1, unofficial]. Weakly connected toxels (vertex/edge contact) result in disjunct segments, which may require post-processing or specific interpretation [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** 4D shape characterization, continuous transmutation visualization, freezeout hypersurface extraction in relativistic heavy-ion physics, and intersection studies of hypersurfaces [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-STEVE (Space-Time-Enclosing Volume Extraction) was introduced as a 4D protomesh-based iso-hypersurface construction algorithm [S1, unofficial]. It was developed to address the limitations of template-based hypersurface generators (like VESTA or MCA) in 4D, where the number of configurations becomes prohibitively large ($2^{16}$ for 4D) [S1, unofficial]. The method was applied to problems in accelerator physics (ion beam transport) and relativistic heavy-ion physics (freezeout hypersurfaces) [S1, unofficial].
+STEVE (Space-Time-Enclosing Volume Extraction) was introduced as a 4D protomesh-based iso-hypersurface construction algorithm [S1, unofficial]. It was developed to address the limitations of template-based methods in higher dimensions, where the number of required configurations grows exponentially [S1, unofficial]. The algorithm was applied to various fields including accelerator physics (ion beam transport phase space), relativistic heavy-ion physics (freezeout hypersurfaces), and continuous transmutation visualization [S1, unofficial]. Initial work was supported by the Department of Energy [S1, unofficial].
 
